--TEST--
Core\Random::bytes draws a raw buffer of the count it is given, two draws differ, and a zero-byte draw throws — spec § 11, `rule:types/bytes` and `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A random answer cannot be asserted, so every line pins an invariant of the
// draw rather than the draw itself.

// Spec § 11: `bytes(uint $count): bytes` counts octets, and `rule:types/bytes` makes
// that buffer a primitive with its own length member rather than an array.
var $key = Core\Random::bytes(16);
echo Core\Bytes::length($key), "\n";
echo Core\Bytes::length(Core\Random::bytes(1)), "\n";

// `rule:types/conversion`: `bytes as string` is *checked*, and an arbitrary draw is not
// valid UTF-8, so the only way to compare two buffers is to render them.
// Two 32-byte draws colliding is a 2^-256 event, not a flake.
var $a = Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Random::bytes(32));
var $b = Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Random::bytes(32));
echo ($a == $b) ? "same" : "differ", "\n";
echo Core\Str::length($a), "\n";
echo Core\Regex::matches($a, '^[0-9a-f]{64}$') ? "hex" : "not hex", "\n";

// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4: an empty buffer is a secret every other empty draw matches, so
// there is no reading of `bytes(0)` worth being total for.
try {
    echo Core\Bytes::length(Core\Random::bytes(0)), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "threw: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
16
1
differ
64
hex
threw: Core\Random::bytes(): a draw of zero bytes is the empty buffer, which is not a secret — draw at least one byte
